er, so maybe not.\n\nB tiles: B8 I have pung, so B8 is safe? Others might need B8 for chow with B6,B7 or B7,B9? B9 is dead, so B8 with B6,B7 is possible. I have B6 and B7, so if I discard B6 or B7, someone might have B8 and need them. But B8 is mostly used.\n\nB1 is terminal, maybe safe because terminals are often discarded early, but B1 has 4 unseen initially, now 2 left? Unshown B1:4, but I have 2 B1, so 2 left unseen. Could be dangerous if someone needs for pung.\n\nGiven scoring patterns, I might aim for a hand with \"Pung of Terminals or Honors\" from B1 pung, or \"All Chows\" if I develop chows. Currently I have one chow T2-4, one pung B8. I could try to get another chow from W2,W3 or B1,B2 or B6,B7. \n\nBest discard candidates: \n- B6: It's a simple tile, but B6 with B7 could form chow with B5 or B8. B5 is rare, B8 is mostly used. So B6 might be relatively safe.\n- W2 or W3: Risky because across player has W chows and might be collecting W suit.\n- T2,T3,T4: I have a chow T2,T3,T4, so I should keep them together. Discarding one would break the chow. But if I discard T4, I still have T2,T3 which can form chow with T1 or T4. But T4 is needed for chow with T2,T3? Actually T2,T3,T4 is a complete chow. If I discard T4, I have T2,T3 which need T1 or T4 to complete chow. So breaking the chow might be bad.\n- B1: I have a pair, discarding one would leave a single, losing a potential pair. But B1 is terminal, which might be valuable for \"Pung of Terminals\" or \"Outside Hand\" but my hand otherwise has simples, so maybe not.\n- B7: I have a pair, similar to B1.\n- B2: single tile, could be discarded.\n\nI think keeping the T2,T3,T4 chow is good because it's a ready meld. So I should not discard T2,T3,T4.\n\nBetween B1, B2, B6, B7, W2, W3: I need to consider tile efficiency and safety.\n\nIf I keep B1 pair and B7 pair, I have two pairs. Then I need two more melds: one is T2-4 chow, and I need one more meld from W2,W3 or B6,B7 or B2 etc.\n\nW2,W3 need W1 or W4. W1 has 4 left unseen, W4 has 3 left, decent chance.\nB6,B7 need B5 or B8. B5 only 1 left, B8 only 1 left, poor chance.\nB1,B2 need B3, which has 3 left, okay chance.\nB2 alone is not helpful.\n\nSo the most promising additional meld is from W2,W3 needing W1/W4, or from B1,B2 needing B3. But B1 is a pair, so if I use B1 in a chow, I break the pair. I could instead aim for B1 pung (draw another B1) and then use B2 with something else.\n\nGiven I have two pairs (B1 and B7), I only need one pair to win. So I could discard one of the pairs to reduce to a single and try to form chows. But discarding a pair reduces my progress.\n\nAlternatively, I could discard a lone tile like B2 or B6 to keep pairs.\n\nLet's evaluate each valid action:\n\n- Play T4: Breaks the chow T2,T3,T4, leaving T2,T3 which need T1 or T4. Not good.\n- Play B6: B6 is lone, no direct connection except with B7 (pair) to form chow with B5/B8. Discarding B6 keeps B7 pair and B1 pair, and keeps W2,W3 for potential chow. Seems okay.\n- Play W3: W3 is part of potential chow with W2 and W1/W4. Discarding W3 reduces chances for that meld. But W3 might be risky for across player.\n- Play T3: Breaks chow.\n- Play T2: Breaks chow.\n- Play W2: Similar to W3.\n- Play B1: Discards one of a pair, leaving single B1. Reduces potential for pung or pair.\n- Play B7: Similar to B1.\n- Play B2: B2 is lone, safe discard? B2 has connections to B1 and B3, but B1 is pair, so maybe discard B2 to keep pairs.\n\nBetween B2 and B6: Both are lone tiles. B2 has 3 unseen, B6 has 3 unseen. But B2 is lower number, maybe more dangerous for chows? Not sure.\n\nConsidering safety: Across player has W chows, so W tiles are dangerous. Previous player has T chow, so T tiles might be dangerous. Next player has F4 pung, so wind tiles dangerous. B tiles might be relatively safe. B2 and B6 are both B suit, and no one has B melds yet. B8 is my pung, so B suit might be less pursued.\n\nFrom game history: Across player played B5, B2, B9, B8, B5 \u2013 so they discarded B tiles. Next player played B9, B3, B6 \u2013 discarded B tiles. Previous played B9 \u2013 discarded B. So B tiles are being discarded. So B2 and B6 might be safe.\n\nI think discarding B6 is reasonable because it's a middle tile with few connections given B5 and B8 are scarce.\n\nBut also consider that B6 is needed for chow with B4,B5 or B5,B7 or B7,B8. B7 is in my hand as pair, so if someone has B5,B7, they need B6. Or if someone has B4,B5, they need B6. B4 has 4 unseen, so possible. But B5 only 1 left, so less likely.\n\nB2 is needed for chow with B1,B3 or B3,B4. B1 I have two, so if someone has B1,B3, they need B2. B3 has 3 left.\n\nBoth have similar risk.\n\nI think keeping the chow T2-4 is important. Keeping pairs B1 and B7 gives me two pairs, so I can wait for one to become pung or discard one later if I get another meld.\n\nSo discard a lone tile: B2 or B6. Choose B6 because B6 is farther from the terminals and might be less useful for others.\n\nAlso, B6 is a simple tile, and discarding it might not help opponents' scoring patterns.\n\nLet's check unshown: B6:3 left, so there are three B6 left. Safe.\n\nThus, I'll discard B6.\n\nReason: Keep the formed chow T2-T4 and maintain two potential pairs (B1 and B7) for flexibility. Discard B6 as a relatively safe lone tile that has low probability of completing opponents' melds given the scarcity of adjacent tiles B5 and B8.\n\nAnswer: Play B6"}
