ssible with one more B7 (unshown B7:3 left).  \n\nUnshown tiles info:  \n- W1:1, W4:3, W5:3, W6:2, etc.  \n- T1:3, T4:1, T5:2, T6:0, T7:2, T8:3, T9:0.  \n- B1:4, B2:3, B3:3, B4:4, B5:0, B6:2, B7:3, B8:0, B9:0.  \n- Honors: F1:1, F2:2, F3:1, F4:1, J1:0, J2:0, J3:1.  \n\nSo for chow possibilities:  \n- W2-W3 needs W1 or W4: W1 has 1 left, W4 has 3 left \u2014 decent.  \n- T2-T3-T3 needs T1 or T4: T1 has 3, T4 has 1 left \u2014 okay.  \n- B1-B1-B2 needs B3: B3 has 3 left \u2014 good.  \n- B6-B7-B7 needs B5 (0 left) or B8 (0 left) \u2014 so no chow possible here, only pung for B7 (3 left) or B6 (2 left) pung unlikely.  \n\nThus, B6 and B7 are relatively isolated. B7 has pung potential (3 left), B6 has pung potential (2 left) but less likely.  \n\nAlso, I should consider defense based on other players' melds:  \n- Next player: melds F4 pung and T4-T5-T6 chow. They have shown F4 and T4-6, so they might be going for honors or mixed hand.  \n- Across player: melds W3-4-5, W6-7-8, T4-5-6 \u2014 they have three chows in W and T, likely going for flush or half-flush with W.  \n- Previous player: melds T7-8-9 chow and W1 pung \u2014 they have terminals and W suit.  \n\nGiven across player has two W chows, they might be collecting W tiles, so discarding W tiles might be risky. But W2 and W3 are relatively safe if W1 and W4 are still available? Actually, they have W3-4-5 and W6-7-8, so they might need W2 for chow? Possibly, but W2 is not directly adjacent to their chows except maybe W2-W3-W4, but they already used W3 in meld. Still, caution.  \n\nBest discards are the isolated tiles with less potential and less risk. B6 is isolated, only pung chance (2 left), no chow chance, and B6 is not shown much in discards (history shows B6 discarded by me and next player earlier). B6 might be relatively safe.  \n\nB7 has pung chance but also isolated; B7 has been discarded by me earlier (Play B7 in my history), so maybe others have B7 too? Unshown B7:3 left, so still some out there.  \n\nB1