ent hand: W2, W3, T2, T3, T3, B1, B1, B2, B7, B7, B7. And one meld: B8 pung.\n\nWe have potential for several chows and pungs. Let's group:\n\n- B7: three copies, can be a pung (but it's in hand, so concealed). That could be a meld.\n- T3: two copies, needs one more for pung.\n- B1: two copies, could be pair or part of chow? B1, B2, B3 chow possible but we have B2 but no B3.\n- W2, W3: could form chow with W1 or W4.\n- T2, T3: could form chow with T1 or T4.\n\nWe also need to consider the game state: many tiles have been discarded. Check unshown tiles counts: T4, T6, T9, B5, B8, B9, F4, J1, J2, J3 are 0 (all used). Also B6:1, F1:1, F2:2, F3:1, etc.\n\nOur hand has many simple tiles (2-8) and no terminals/honors except maybe B7 is simple. We have no honors. So patterns like All Simples (2 points) are possible. Also, we have three suits, so maybe All Types (6 points) if we include winds/dragons, but we have no honors currently. So All Types not possible unless we get honor meld. But we have B8 pung which is simple, and B7 pung simple, so maybe aim for All Simples + maybe All Pungs (6 points) if we get four pungs. Currently we have B8 pung (exposed) and potential B7 pung (concealed). We need two more pungs. We have pairs: T3 pair, B1 pair. Could try to pung T3 or B1. But T3: unshown T3 count is 3? Actually unshown T3:3, so two more available. B1: unshown B1:4, so two more available. But we also have T2 and W2, W3 etc. Could also form chows.\n\nWe need at least 8 points to win. So we need to plan scoring patterns. Possible patterns from current hand:\n\n- All Simples (2 points): hand has no terminals/honors. B8 and B7 are simple (6-8). T2, T3, W2, W3, B1, B2 are all simple. So if we win with this hand, we get All Simples (2).\n- Concealed Hand (2 points): if we win by drawing our own tile and all melds concealed except maybe B8 is exposed, so not concealed. Actually B8 is exposed, so Concealed Hand not possible.\n- Self-Drawn (1 point) if we draw winning tile.\n- Pung of Terminals or Honors (1 point each): none currently.\n- All Pungs (6 points): if we have four pungs/kongs. We have B8 pung, potential B7 pung, need two more pungs. Could pung T3 and B1. That would give four pungs: B8, B7, T3, B1. All are simple, so also All Simples. Total points: All Pungs (6) + All Simples (2) = 8 points exactly. That's a viable plan. But we need to get T3 and B1 pungs. Unshown T3:3, B1:4, so possible. But we need to draw the third copies or claim discards. However, claiming discards would expose melds, but All Pungs allows exposed pungs. So that's okay.\n\nAlternatively, we could go for chows. For example, we have W2, W3 needing W1 or W4 for chow. Unshown W1:1, W4:3. T2, T3 needing T1 or T4 for chow. Unshown T1:3, T4:0 (none left! T4 is 0 unshown). So T4 is exhausted (all used in melds by others). So T2,T3 cannot form chow with T4. They could form chow with T1,T2,T3 if we get T1. But we have T2 and two T3, so T1,T2,T3 chow would use one T2 and one T3, leaving one T3 extra. Alternatively, T2,T3,T4 chow impossible. So chow route for T-suit is limited.\n\nB1,B2 could form chow with B3. Unshown B3:3, so possible.\n\nBut if we go for chows, we might get All Chows (2 points) plus All Simples (2) = 4 points, not enough. Need more patterns.\n\nThus, the pung plan seems more promising to reach 8 points.\n\nNow, we need to discard one tile. We want to keep tiles that help form pungs. Currently, we have:\n\n- B7: three copies, already a pung (we can consider it a meld, but it's in hand, so we can declare it as concealed pung later when we win, or maybe we need to keep it as a potential meld). Actually, we have three B7, so it's a complete pung. So that's our second meld (concealed). So we have two melds: B8 pung (exposed) and B7 pung (concealed). We need two more melds and a pair.\n\nWe have pairs: T3 (two copies) and B1 (two copies). So we could use one as pair and the other as pung. Or both as pungs and find a separate pair.\n\nIf we use T3 as pair, we need pung for B1 and one more meld (could be chow or pung). If we use B1 as pair, we need pung for T3 and one more meld.\n\nWe also have single tiles: W2, W3, T2, B2. These could form chows or become pairs.\n\nLet's list all tiles after considering B7 as pung (set aside). Remaining hand: W2, W3, T2, T3, T3, B1, B1, B2. That's 8 tiles. We need two melds and a pair from these 8.\n\nOptions:\n\n1. Aim for T3 pung and B1 pung: need one more T3 and one more B1. Then we have W2, W3, T2, B2 left \u2013 we need to form a pair from these. Could pair W2/W3? They are not same. Could pair T2 or B2? We have only one each. So we would need to draw a matching tile to make a pair. That's possible. So if we get T3 and B1 pungs, we have two singles left that need to become a pair.\n\n2. Aim for one pung and one chow. For example, T3 pung (need one T3) and a chow from W2,W3 + W1/W4, or T2,T3 + T1, or B1,B2 + B3. Then we have remaining tiles to form pair.\n\nGiven unshown counts: T3:3 available, B1:4 available, so pungs are feasible. W1:1, W4:3, T1:3, B3:3.\n\nDiscard choice: we want to discard a tile that is less useful for forming pungs or chows, and that doesn't ruin potential.\n\nLet's evaluate each valid action:\n\n- Play W3: Discard W3. Keeps W2, which could pair with another W2? Unshown W2:2, so possible. But W2,W3 could have been chow with W1 or W4. Discarding W3 loses that chow option. But chow may not be needed if we go for pungs. W3 is a single, not part of a pair currently. So maybe okay.\n\n- Play T3: Discard one T3. We have two T3, so discarding one leaves one T3, breaking the pair. That would make it harder to get T3 pung. But we could still get T3 pung if we get two more T3, which is less likely. So discarding T3 is bad if we want T3 pung.\n\n- Play T2: Discard T2. T2 is a single, could be used for chow with T1,T3? Not directly. Could become pair if we get another T2. Unshown T2:3. But T2 is less useful than T3 pair. So maybe okay.\n\n- Play W2: Similar to W3.\n\n- Play B1: Discard B1. We have two B1, discarding one breaks the pair, making B1 pung harder. Not good.\n\n- Play B7: Discard B7. We have three B7, discarding one leaves two B7, breaking the pung. That would lose our ready-made meld. Very bad.\n\n- Play B2: Discard B2. B2 is single, could be used for chow with B1,B3, or become pair. Unshown B2:3. So maybe okay.\n\nSo clearly, discarding B7 or B1 or T3 would break existing sets. We should keep B7 pung, T3 pair, B1 pair. So discard candidates: W2, W3, T2, B2.\n\nAmong these, which is least useful? We have two suits with potential chows: W2,W3 can become chow with W1 or W4. T2,T3 can become chow with T1 (since T4 is gone). B1,B2 can become chow with B3. So all have chow potential. But if we aim for pungs, we don't need chows. For pungs, we need to keep T3 and B1 pairs. The singles W2, W3, T2, B2 are only useful if they become pairs to complete the hand (since after T3 pung and B1 pung, we need a pair from remaining). So we need to keep tiles that can easily form pairs. Which pair is easiest? Look at unshown counts:\n\n- W2: unshown 2\n- W3: unshown 2\n- T2: unshown 3\n- B2: unshown 3\n\nAlso, we have one copy of each in hand. So the probability of drawing another to make a pair is similar for all, but T2 and B2 have more available. However, we also need to consider that if we discard a tile, we might make it harder for others to win, but that's secondary.\n\nWe also need to consider the game history: many tiles have been played. Look at discards: W6, T9, F3, T4, W9, T1, B9, W7, J2, B7, Peng B8, etc. B7 has been played by us? Actually in our history: \"Play B7\" earlier? Wait, our history shows \"Play B7\" as one of our discards? Actually history: \"Play W6, Play T9, Play F3, Play T4, Play W9, Play T1, Play B9, Play W7, Play J2, Play B7, Peng B8, Play B5, Play B6, Play T4, Play T6, Play B6.\" So we discarded B7 earlier. That means other players know we discarded B7, so they might think we don't need B7. But now we have three B7, so we could have drawn them later. That's fine.\n\nAlso, note that the next player has a kong of F4 and a chow T4,T5,T6. The across player has many chows. The previous player has chow T7,T8,T9 and pung W1.\n\nGiven we want to go for All Pungs + All Simples, we should keep the pairs and the pung. So discard one of the singles. Among singles, which is most dangerous to discard? Look at melds of others: next player has F4 kong and T4,T5,T6 chow \u2013 no direct relation. Across player has W3,W4,W5 chow, W6,W7,W8 chow, T4,T5,T6 chow. So they have W3 in a chow, but that's already used. They might need W2 for chow? Possibly. But they haven't shown interest in W2. Previous player has T7,T8,T9 chow and W1 pung. They might need W2 for chow? Unlikely.\n\nAlso, consider that T2 and T3 are close to T1 and T4, but T4 is exhausted. So discarding T2 might be safe.\n\nB2 is simple, and B1,B2,B3 chow possible for others, but B3 is still available (3 copies). So discarding B2 might be riskier if someone is collecting Bamboo.\n\nBut overall, we need to choose one. Let's think about hand shape: if we discard W2 or W3, we lose the possibility of a W-chow, but we are not aiming for chows. If we discard T2, we lose T-chow possibility, but T-chow is limited due to T4 gone. If we discard B2, we lose B-chow possibility.\n\nMaybe discard W3 because it's the highest single without obvious connections? But W2 and W3 are symmetric.\n\nAnother factor: we have two T3, so if we discard T2, we keep T3 pair, and T2 is not needed for pung. T2 could become a pair if we draw another, but we already have T3 pair, so we might not need T2 pair. Actually, after forming T3 pung and B1 pung, we need a pair from the remaining two tiles. If we keep W2 and W3, they are different, so we would need to draw a tile that matches either to make a pair. If we keep T2 and B2, same issue. So ideally, we want to keep two copies of the same tile to have a ready pair. But we don't have that among singles. So we need to draw a pair. So we should keep tiles that have more available copies to increase chance of drawing a match.\n\nT2: unshown 3, B2: unshown 3, W2: unshown 2, W3: unshown 2. So T2 and B2 have more available. So we should keep T2 and B2, and discard one of W2 or W3.\n\nBetween W2 and W3, which to discard? W3 might be slightly more dangerous because across player has W3 in a chow, but they already have it, so they might not need another. Also, previous player discarded W2 earlier? History: previous player played W2? Actually, previous player history: \"Play W2\" and \"Play W2\" again? They played W2 twice? Look: \"Play J3, Play F3, Chi T8, Play T9, Play F2, Play J2, Play T2, Play B9, Play T6, Play W2, Play J1, Peng W1, Play B8, Play W2, Play B5, Play J3.\" So they discarded W2 twice. So W2 might be safer to discard because it's been discarded already. But if we discard W2, we keep W3. Or vice versa.\n\nAlso, note that our hand has no terminals/honors, so we are safe from giving away big hands.\n\nI think discarding W2 or W3 is fine. Let's choose W3 because W2 might be slightly safer due to previous discards.\n\nBut let's double-check: if we discard W3, we keep W2. Then hand: W2, T2, T3 pair, B1 pair, B2, and B7 pung. That's okay.\n\nAlternatively, if we discard W2, we keep W3.\n\nI'll choose to discard W3.\n\nReason: We aim for All Pungs and All Simples, which requires keeping the B7 pung, T3 pair, and B1 pair. Discarding W3 preserves these while removing a single tile with fewer remaining copies. W3 has been used in a chow by another player, making it relatively safe.\n\nAnswer: Play W3"}
