4 has 3 left, possible. B6 has 1 left. So the best candidates for a pung are B4 (3 left) or T8 (2 left) or B2 (2 left). But B2 is simple, okay.  \n\nSo strategy: discard terminals/honors first, keep simple tiles that can form pungs. Currently, B1 is the only terminal in hand. So discard B1.  \n\nBut wait, I have B7 pung, which is simple, good. T3 pung simple. So if I discard B1, my hand becomes all simples. Then I need one more pung and a pair. I have T8, B2, B4, B6 as singles. Which one to discard? I should keep tiles that have more copies left to increase chance of drawing a pair/pung. B4 has 3 left, B2 has 2, T8 has 2, B6 has 1. So B4 is best, then B2 and T8, then B6 worst. So I should discard B6? But B6 is simple, and only 1 left, so unlikely to complete a pung. So discard B6 first? But I need to consider immediate discard. My valid actions are Play B4, B6, T8, T3, B1, B7, B2.  \n\nI cannot play T3 or B7 because they are part of pungs? Actually, I have three T3, so I could discard one, but that would break the pung. Better to keep the pung for All Pungs. So discard among B1, B2, B4, B6, T8.  \n\nI should discard B1 first to move towards All Simples. Then later discard B6 because it has only 1 left. But in this turn, I must choose one.  \n\nAlso, consider the game state: other players have melds. Next player has a kong of F4, and chows. Across player has chows. Previous player has chows and a pung of W1. Many tiles have been played. Looking at history, many terminals and honors have been played. B1 has been played once by me earlier? In my history: Play B1 (last action). So B1 is dangerous? But I have three B1 left in wall? Unshown tiles show B1:3 left. So there are three B1 left. Not too dangerous.  \n\nBut discarding B1 might give someone a pung? Possibly, but no one has shown B1 melds.  \n\nBetter to discard B1 to improve hand value.  \n\nSo my choice: Play B1.  \n\nBut let's check if there is a better discard for efficiency. If I discard B1, I keep B2, B4, B6, T8. These are all simple and can form pairs. But B6 has only 1 left, so it's a dead tile. Discarding B6 now might be better because it's less useful. But if I discard B6, I still have B1 terminal, which prevents All Simples. So I need to discard B1 eventually. So discard B1 now.  \n\nAlso, note that I have a kong of B8, which is simple. So All Simples is possible if I discard B1.  \n\nThus, I choose Play B1.  \n\nReason: Discarding the terminal B1 moves me towards All Simples (2 points) combined with All Pungs (6 points) for a total of 8 points, which is the minimum to win. Keeping the pungs of T3 and B7 and aiming to draw another pung from the remaining simple tiles (B2, B4, T8) gives a good chance to complete a winning hand with adequate points.  \n\nAnswer: Play B1"}
